      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright (c) 2008 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": "Although a low level of high density lipoprotein-cholesterol (HDL-C) is an important risk factor for coronary heart disease, few available agents are capable of significantly increasing HDL-C. This multicenter study demonstrated that administration of pitavastatin, a new HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor, significantly and persistently increased HDL-C (from 36.0+/-5.9 to 40.5+/-9.1 mg/dL: p<0.001) and apolipoprotein A-I levels (from 108.4+/-18.0 to 118.7+/-19.3 mg/dL: p<0.01) in 43 hypercholesterolemic patients with low HDL-C over the course of 12 months of treatment. This suggests that pitavastatin may contribute to reduction in coronary heart disease."
      },
